DESC = "Male voice in his 30s with an American accent, a warm natural documentary storyteller, conversational and sincere, clear mid pitch, medium pacing with natural pauses, engaged and curious, not acting."
PARAS = [
    # COLD OPEN
    ("Free shipping over thirty five dollars makes shoppers spend about nine percent more. Harvard Business School researchers found that, after studying more than two million orders.", 0.35, 701),
    ("Free shipping isn't free. Somebody pays for the truck. And the stores have two tricks that make sure it's mostly you. One is the free shipping line. The other is four easy payments.", 0.5, 602),
    # TRICK 1: THE THRESHOLD
    ("Let's start with the line. You're buying something for twenty eight dollars. Shipping is six. Or, you could add one more thing and get to thirty five.", 0.3, 603),
    ("Lots of people add the thing. And here's what the researchers noticed. People don't land right on thirty five. They overshoot. The store still pays for shipping, but now it's selling more stuff on every order.", 0.5, 604),
    # TRICK 2: MOVING THE LINE
    ("And the store wins even when you walk away. In that study, people placed about six percent fewer orders once free shipping needed a minimum. But the bigger baskets made up for it, and the store's profit on products went up. Fewer trips, bigger carts.", 0.5, 621),
    ("Trick number two. Move the line, and watch what people do.", 0.3, 605),
    ("In twenty thirteen, Amazon raised its free shipping minimum from twenty five dollars to thirty five. In twenty sixteen, it raised it again, to forty nine, for anyone without Prime. Prime was ninety nine dollars a year back then, and it came with no minimum at all.", 0.3, 606),
    ("The message was simple. Stop doing the math. Just join.", 0.5, 607),
    # TRICK 3: THE MEMBERSHIP
    ("Trick number three is the membership itself. Once you've paid for free shipping, every order feels free. So you order more.", 0.3, 608),
    ("One research firm, CIRP, estimated that Prime members spent about thirteen hundred dollars a year on Amazon, compared with about seven hundred for everyone else. Same store. Almost double the spending.", 0.5, 609),
    # RE-HOOK / TRICK 4: PAY IN FOUR
    ("That's the shipping trick. The payment trick is even better, because it makes a two hundred dollar jacket feel like a fifty dollar jacket.", 0.3, 610),
    ("Pay in four. Buy now, pay later. Klarna, Afterpay, Affirm. No interest, they say. So who's paying for it? The store is. These companies charge the store a fee on every sale, and one report found it can be as much as three times what a credit card costs.", 0.3, 611),
    ("So why would a store pay that? Because it works. When Stripe tested it across more than a hundred and fifty thousand checkouts, businesses saw up to fourteen percent more revenue when pay later was an option. And more than two thirds of that pay later spending was brand new sales. Money people would not have spent otherwise.", 0.5, 612),
    # TRICK 5: THE FEELING
    ("Trick number five is what it does to your head. Fifty dollars today feels small. Three more fifty dollar payments are a problem for future you.", 0.3, 613),
    ("In a LendingTree survey, nearly seventy percent of pay later users said they spend more than they would if they had to pay everything up front. Forty two percent said they'd paid late at least once, and almost a quarter said they regretted it. And in a Federal Reserve study, more than half of people who used it said it was the only way they could afford the purchase.", 0.5, 614),
    # WHAT TO DO
    ("The Federal Reserve also compared credit records. People who used pay later had, on average, more than three times as many accounts gone late or into collections as people who didn't. That doesn't mean pay later caused it. But it tells you who these loans are working on.", 0.5, 622),
    ("So what do you do? You don't have to quit online shopping. You just play the game better.", 0.3, 615),
    ("When you're short of a free shipping line, only add something you were already going to buy this month. Otherwise, pay the shipping. It's usually cheaper than the stuff you'd add.", 0.3, 616),
    ("If you pay for a membership, check once a year how much you actually ordered, and ask yourself if you'd have bought all of it without it.", 0.3, 617),
    ("And treat four easy payments as what it is. A loan. If you can't pay the full price today, that's the answer.", 0.5, 618),
    ("Free shipping and four easy payments are a deal for the store first. Make them a deal for you too.", 0.5, 619),
    ("We break down one business trick every day. Subscribe, and tell us which checkout trick gets you every time.", 0.0, 620),
]
